1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxc4 $5 {It is strange that this very natural capture has been more or less completely neglected by theory. As far as I can see, it gives Black a highly satisfactory game; g5 is not necessarily a good square for the bishop in the Queen’s Gambit Accepted.} 5. e4 (5. e3 {is more solid.}) 5... c5 $1 6. d5 $6 ({The most aggressive move, but already a mistake. Safer was} 6. Nf3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Be7 8. Ndb5 a6 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. O-O-O+ Bd7 11. Nd6 Bxd6 12. Rxd6 Kc7 {with approximate equality, Timoshchenko- Crouch, Douai 1991.}) 6... Be7 $1 {Now the threat of ...Nxd5 is awkward.} 7. Bf4 ({After} 7. f4 exd5 8. e5 $2 (8. exd5 O-O 9. Bxc4 h6 $15) 8... d4 $1 9. exf6 gxf6 10. Ne4 fxg5 11. Bxc4 O-O 12. fxg5 b5 $1 13. Qf3 bxc4 14. Nf6+ Bxf6 15. gxf6 Qd6 $1 {Black was already winning in Raicevic-Blatny, European Club Cup 1989; the white centre is non-existent.}) 7... Nxe4 $1 ({Far stronger than Blatny’s suggested} 7... Qa5 {; Black gains three strong central pawns for the piece and is already better.}) 8. Nxe4 exd5 {Compare this with a system first played in Tal-Karolyi, Tbilisi 1986: 1 d4 d5 2 c4 dxc4 3 Nf3 c5 4 d5 Nf6 5 4 Nc3 e6 6 e4 Nxe4?! 7 Nxe4 exd5. Because of the wasted bishop move (Bg5-f4) Black is already a tempo up in the Crouch game. This tempo makes a big difference; in the Karolyi move order, after 8 Nc3 Be6 9 Ng5 Nc6 10 g3! Be7 11 Nxe6 fxe6 12 Bh3 Kf713 0-0 Nd4 14 Be3 Bf6 15 Ne2 Nxe2+ 16 Qxe2 Qe7 17 Rad1, Black’s pawn centre was under very severe pressure in Lukacs-Nemeth, Hungary 1987.} 9. Ng3 O-O 10. Nf3 Bf6 11. Qd2 Nc6 $2 ({A mistake after half an hour’s thought. Black is much too fascinated with his pawn centre and plays for a slow, too slow, grind. I should have played} 11... Qb6 $1 {with virtually a winning position already, for example} 12. Qxd5 (12. O-O-O Nc6) 12... Be6 13. Qd2 Qxb2 {(or maybe first 13...Rd8) with mayhem on the queenside. I completely underestimated the strength of this.}) 12. Be2 Bg4 13. O-O Bxf3 $2 ({A second weak move after which Black is worse. I should have advanced on the queenside with} 13... a5 {or 13...b5.}) 14. Bxf3 Nd4 15. Bg4 a5 ({Black takes a dislike to} 15... Ne6 16. Bxe6 $1 fxe6 17. Rfe1 {, with a pawn structure similar to the Lukacs-Nemeth game; after a timely Be5 White has pressure.}) 16. Nh5 a4 17. Rae1 Nc6 {An admission of failure, but the e5 square was weak.} 18. Re3 a3 $2 ({Better} 18... Bd4 $14 {Now White has a forced win.}) 19. Bc7 $1 Qxc7 20. Nxf6+ gxf6 21. Bf5 $1 ({Only way!} 21. Rg3 $2 Ne5 $1) ({or} 21. Rh3 Qe5 22. Qh6 Rfe8 {both hold.}) 21... Rfe8 (21... Ne5 22. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 (22... Kg7 23. Rh3 Ng4 24. Bf5) 23. Rh3+ Kg6 $1 24. f4 $1 Rh8 25. Rg3+ Kh7 26. fxe5 Qxe5 27. Rf4 {forces mate.}) 22. Rg3+ Kf8 23. Qh6+ Ke7 24. Re1+ Ne5 25. f4 Ra6 26. fxe5 fxe5 27. Qg5+ $2 ({White was in extreme time trouble after spending over an hour for his first eight moves.} 27. Qh4+ $1 {wins, for example} Rf6 (27... Kd6 28. Qf6+) (27... Kf8 28. Bxh7) 28. Rf3 Rd8 29. Ref1 Rdd6 30. Be6) 27... f6 28. Qh6 Kd8 29. Rg7 Re7 30. Rg8+ Re8 31. Rg7 Re7 32. Rg8+ Re8 33. Rxe8+ $2 {Wiser to take the draw. Curiously, three of my Hastings opponents lost to me after ignoring certain draws. Ketevan Arakhamia snatched a poisoned pawn in the endgame, much as in Spassky-Fischer, 1st match game 1972, and was furious with herself afterwards. I had to win the tournament to get her to speak to me again! Ilya Gurevich avoided a draw by repetition of moves four (!) times in the opening, and then went for a hazardous attack which lost. In either case, this was my opponent’s only loss of the tournament. And now a third time.} Kxe8 34. Bxh7 axb2 {?A position for the connoisseur of unusual pawn structures! One can deduce that Black has made at least five pawn captures. Furthermore, when White’s a- pawn and Black’s f-pawn disappear, there will be eight passed pawns on the board. The feature of real practical significance is that six of these passed pawns will be Black’s. As the next few moves will show, the black king is safe.} 35. Bg6+ Kd7 36. Qg7+ $6 (36. Bf5+ {keeps better piece coordination, and leaves the game unclear.}) 36... Kc6 $1 37. Qxf6+ Kb5 38. Qf5 Rxa2 39. h4 Ra1 40. Qf2 Ka6 $6 ({Overlooking White’s next move, but the position is still won.} 40... Rxe1+ 41. Qxe1 e4 {is simpler.}) 41. Bb1 Qa5 $1 {With the idea of ...Qc3 and ...Qc1.} 42. Qf1 Qd2 43. h5 Ka7 44. Rd1 Qe3+ 45. Kh1 c3 $6 (45... e4 $1 {, although} 46. Kh2 {is still tricky.}) 46. Qb5 e4 47. Kh2 Qf4+ 48. Kh3 $2 ({Losing quickly.} 48. Kh1 {gives Black a bit more to worry about, but} Qd6 {still looks good.}) 48... Qf5+ 49. Kh4 ({As there is nothing in} 49. g4 Qf3+ 50. Kh4 Qxd1 51. Qxc5+ Ka6) 49... d4 50. Bc2 Qf6+ 51. Kh3 Qb6 52. Qf1 Rxd1 53. Qxd1 d3 {A sadistic final position. (Sources: bulletin; CHESS, March 1992, p27)} 0-1

